{"answerArea":{"calculator":false,"chi2Table":false,"periodicTable":false,"tTable":false,"zTable":false},"hints":[{"content":"We are asked to find the *second* derivative of a parametric function.\n\nRecall that the *first* derivative of a function defined parametrically by the equations $x=u(t)$ and $y=v(t)$ is found with the following rule:\n\n$\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{dy}{dt}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{dx}{dt}\\right)}=\\dfrac{v'(t)}{u'(t)}$\n\nThen, the *second* derivative is found with this following rule:\n\n$\\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}=\\dfrac{\\dfrac{d}{dt}\\left(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{dx}{dt}\\right)}=\\dfrac{\\dfrac{d}{dt}\\left(\\dfrac{v'(t)}{u'(t)}\\right)}{u'(t)}$","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"Let's start by finding $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}$.\n\n$\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\cos^3(t)$\n\n[[☃ explanation 1]]","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{"explanation 1":{"alignment":"default","graded":true,"options":{"explanation":"We are given that $x=\\tan(t)$ and $y=\\sin(t)+\\dfrac\\pi2$:\n\n$\\begin{align}\n\\dfrac{dy}{dx}\u0026=\\dfrac{\\dfrac{d}{dt}[\\sin(t)+\\dfrac\\pi2]}{\\dfrac{d}{dt}[\\tan(t)]}\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=\\dfrac{\\cos(t)}{\\sec^2(t)}\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=\\dfrac{\\cos(t)}{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\cos^2(t)}\\right)}\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=\\cos^3(t)\n\\end{align}$","hidePrompt":"Got it, thanks!","showPrompt":"Show entire calculation.","static":false,"widgets":{}},"static":false,"type":"explanation","version":{"major":0,"minor":0}}}},{"content":"Now we can find $\\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}$.\n\n$\\begin{align}\n\\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}\u0026=\\dfrac{\\dfrac{d}{dt}\\left(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{dx}{dt}\\right)}\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=\\dfrac{\\dfrac{d}{dt}\\left[\\cos^3(t)\\right]}{\\dfrac{d}{dt}[\\tan(t)]}\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=\\dfrac{\\left[3\\cos^2(t)\\right]\\cdot\\dfrac{d}{dt}[\\cos(t)]}{\\sec^2(x)}\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=\\dfrac{\\left[3\\cos^2(t)\\right]\\cdot[-\\sin(t)]}{\\sec^2(x)}\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=\\dfrac{-3\\cos^2(t)\\sin(t)}{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\cos^2(x)}\\right)}\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=-3\\cos^4(t)\\sin(t)\n\\end{align}$\n","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"In conclusion, $\\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}=-3\\cos^4(t)\\sin(t)$.","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}}],"itemDataVersion":{"major":0,"minor":1},"question":{"content":"A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x=\\tan(t)$ and $y=\\sin(t)+\\dfrac\\pi2$.\n\n**What is $\\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}$ in terms of $t$ ?**\n\n[[☃ radio 1]]","images":{},"widgets":{"radio 1":{"alignment":"default","graded":true,"options":{"choices":[{"content":"$-3\\cos^4(t)\\sin(t)$","correct":true},{"content":"$\\cos^3(t)\\sin(t)$","correct":false},{"content":"$\\cos^5(t)$","correct":false,"isNoneOfTheAbove":false},{"content":"$-\\sin(t)\\tan^3(t)$","correct":false,"isNoneOfTheAbove":false}],"deselectEnabled":false,"displayCount":null,"hasNoneOfTheAbove":false,"multipleSelect":false,"onePerLine":false,"randomize":true},"static":false,"type":"radio","version":{"major":1,"minor":0}}}}}
